            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>

&lt;b&gt;Observed Air Leakage:&lt;/b&gt;



Although we did identify some annoying areas of air leakage, you have a large house and the overall amount of leakage volume is just slightly more than it should be. Here are some of the main air leakage areas we identified:



- The ceiling lights on the main floor (air leakage into the attic). It would be good to air seal these electrical penetration from inside the main floor, rather than from inside the attic space.



- The 3 bathroom fans, especially the 2 on the main floor (these 2 are older units that do not seal well and have alot of air leakage through them). You should consider upgrading this fan to a new Energy Star model that have good butterfly dampers, uses less electricity &amp; operates alot quieter.



- The 6" chimney for the current furnace (this chimney would be eliminated with an upgrade to an Energy Star high efficiency furnace)



- Some of the frames for the older windows (this would be eliminated with upgrades to Energy Star windows for these older units - including the patio door).



- There is some leakage through electrical plugs &amp; switches located in exterior walls of the house. As a shorter term solution, you could install the specially designed foam gaskets behind the cover plates. (For the longer term, this leakage should be reduced significantly in conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, that incorporates a full exterior &amp; well-sealed air barrier - such as Tyvek house wrap)



- The attic hatch requires weather-stripping or could be sealed with a removable caulking (eg: Draft Attack).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

For a modest 10% reduction in air leakage, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 82&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;2.7 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 500 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must have a second home energy evaluation, where the air leakage reduction will be measured. For a full rebate, the rebate application, with a copy of the first page of both home energy evaluation reports, must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of the second energy evaluation. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>

The exterior walls of your house are 2x4 (R12), which is much less than the minimum R30 - R35 that new housing in Yellowknife is recommended to achieve. For your longer term planning, I have modeled the expected energy savings if you were to add 4" (R20) of rigid foam insulation to the exterior of these walls in conjunction with a future exterior siding upgrade.



From the exterior of your house, these main floor walls extend seamlessly down over the header space (between the basement and upper floor) and the pony walls that represent the upper wood frame walls of the basement that sit upon the lower concrete walls. I would suggest that the additional R20 of exterior rigid insulation extend all the way down to cover the header space and lower basement pony walls.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of the exterior walls of the house (main floor, header space &amp; basement pony walls) with an additional R20 insulation, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 846&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;28.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 2,329 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, you must insulate a minimum of 20 percent of the total exterior wall area and add a minimum insulation value of RSI 0.7 (R-3.8). The grant amount differs according to the additional insulation that you install. The grant is also pro-rated based on the percentage of wall area that is insulated compared to the total wall area after the retrofits. You must apply and receive pre-approval for an EEIP insulation rebate.



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website:

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</mainWalls>
                <ceilings />
                <exposedFloors>

The right side of your house has 2' of exposed floor, and it is difficult to evaluate how much insulation may be in there. In conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade, I would suggest you investigate (from the underside) how much insulation is in there &amp; then fill the complete floor cavity with insulation. I would also suggest adding 4" (R20) of rigid foam insulation to the underside of this exposed floor.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By increasing 100% of this exposed floor with an additional R20 insulation, the estimated savings are only about $17&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs. Although these estimated annual savings may not be great, this upgrade is a natural addition to doing the exterior wall insulation &amp; will have spin off benefits - the floor will feel warmer &amp; the potential dampness staining on the subfloor (that you noticed during a flooring upgrade) should no longer occur.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</exposedFloors>
                <foundation />
                <windows>

You have some older windows in your house that are not triple pane with low e coatings and argon gas fill. These windows include all 4 of the basement windows, the windows and patio door along the back of the main floor and the older wood window in your dining room.



For your longer term energy efficiency planning (and in conjunction with an exterior wall insulation upgrade), I have modeled the potential energy savings associated with upgrading these older windows to ENERGY STAR® qualified windows rated for climate zone 3. In addition, I recommend triple paned, double low-E, argon gas filled, hinged windows.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt; 

By replacing the older windows with ENERGY STAR® qualified units rated for climate zone 3, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 245&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;8.2 GJ&lt;/b&gt;. There are currently no rebates available for installing new windows from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>

The complex world of Building Science can be summed up in one statement ............ "Build Tight &amp; Ventilate Right". New house construction has a consistent focus on this theme and almost all new housing in Yellowknife includes a Heat Recovery Ventilation (HRV) system. When retrofitting existing houses, it is important to reduce excess air leakage (uncontrolled heat &amp; moisture loss) while being aware when a house may become under-ventilated and require some additional ventilation.



During my evaluation of your home, it appears your house is only moderated "over ventilated" and natural air leakage from your home will be reduced in conjunction with some of the recommended upgrades (ie: high efficiency furnace, new ESTAR bathroom fans, exterior wall insulation that includes an air barrier house wrap, new ESTAR windows &amp; patio door, air sealing around the ceiling lights, etc.). Should the house become slightly under-ventilated, you could either increase the amount of exhaust-only ventilation, or install an HRV that is connected to the furnace duct system - for distribution. The HRV would recover about 65% of the heat from the outgoing air and transfer it to the incoming air (ie: the current fresh air pipe connected to the top of your furnace would now be connected into the HRV to pre-heat that incoming air). 



Proper ventilation protects a home from unwanted toxins, pollutants, and odors and can also help protect the overall structure of a home or building by eliminating excess moisture from the air. An energy-efficient heat recovery ventilator is one of the best ways to control indoor air quality.



Depending on the number of energy efficiency upgrades you complete, I may recommend that you have an ENERGY STAR® rated Heat Recovery Ventilator (HRV) system designed and installed by an individual who holds current certification from an organization, such as HRAI. Let's continue to talk about this.



&lt;b&gt;Rebates:&lt;/b&gt;

There are currently no rebates available for installing an HRV from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>

Your house currently has a 180 litre electric hot water tank. Should you consider a switch to a propane furnace, we talked about an opportunity for you to consider replacing this electric tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with an energy factor of 0.92 or higher (I modeled one that is 0.95).



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your hot electric hot water tank with an ENERGY STAR® qualified instantaneous condensing gas-fired water heater with a thermal efficiency of 0.95, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,086&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, and qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 1,250 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

</HotWaterSystem>
                <HeatingSystem>

Your current oil furnace is not that old, however it is a very low efficiency model - only rated at 80% Annual Fuel Utilization Efficiency (AFUE). There is no dedicated combustion air pipe in the furnace room, however there are 2 vent grates through the furnace room walls (one high &amp; one low) that allow the furnace to draw combustion air from inside the basement. I also located a 5" fresh air supply pipe connected at the top of the furnace to bring in fresh air to the house (whenever the furnace or a bathroom exhaust fan or the dryer is operating), and the furnace fan then evenly distributes the fresh air throughout the house.



This furnace also has a humidifier installed &amp; I rarely see these in Yellowknife on houses that also have an outside fresh air pipe connected to the furnace. We talked about this near the end of my evaluation, and you mentioned that you are not operating the humidifier.



Although the furnace is relatively new and the oil tank has also been upgraded - the furnace is still very low efficiency so it will cost you alot of extra heating $$ for many years to come. I have modeled an upgrade to an ENERGY STAR® qualified high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with a minimum efficiency of 95% AFUE, including a high efficiency ECM motor to circulate the heat. This furnace would direct vent out the side wall of the house, eliminating the need for the chimney &amp; drawing its' combustion air from outside rather than from the basement. 



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By replacing your current oil furnace with a high-efficiency condensing propane furnace with an efficiency of 95% AFUE, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 1,303&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs, decrease your annual energy usage by &lt;b&gt;37.0 GJ&lt;/b&gt;., and you would qualify for up to &lt;b&gt;$ 750 in rebates&lt;/b&gt; from the GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program. 



&lt;b&gt;To qualify for a GNWT Energy Efficiency Incentive Program rebate, the rebate application and sales receipt must be received within &lt;u&gt;three months&lt;/u&gt; of purchase date. For a full rebate, qualifying products must be purchased from participating NWT vendors. 



For more information and rebate application forms, please visit our website: 

http://aea.nt.ca/programs/energy-efficiency-incentive-program&lt;/b&gt;



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



</HeatingSystem>
                <Renewables />
                <AdditionalComments>

&lt;b&gt;Circulating the Pellet Stove Heat&lt;/b&gt;



Your house currently has a wood pellet stove in the basement, that draws its' combustion air from inside the basement. We talked about having a dedicated outside combustion air pipe connected to this pellet stove.



We also talked about ways that you could efficiently circulate some of that pellet stove heat throughout your house. Right now there is just a hole from the basement ceiling that goes to the upper floor &amp; this just doesn't do a great job.



We talked about an opportunity for you to have a hole cut into the return air plenum (just outside the basement bedroom door), and using the high efficiency motor of a new propane furnace to draw the warm air off the basement ceiling &amp; circulate it throughout the whole house. We talked about having some vent dampers on the 2 return air pipes in the basement.

    - when you are not using the pellet stove, you want the low wall return air open &amp; the ceiling one closed.

    - when you are using the pellet stove, you want the low wall return closed and the ceiling one open.



Based on your current house insulation &amp; air leakage levels, I have modeled the estimated energy savings (net of oil saved &amp; cost of wood pellets purchased) if you were to use the wood pellet stove to offset about 50% of the heat load of your house.



&lt;b&gt;Savings:&lt;/b&gt;

By using your wood pellet stove to offset 50% of the heat load of your house, you could potentially &lt;b&gt;save $ 715&lt;/b&gt; per year in energy costs



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Ventilation&lt;/b&gt;



Your home is currently ventilated by using the three bathroom exhaust fans and the clothes dryer (the kitchen range hood fan does not exhaust to the outside), where fresh air is drawn back into the house through the 5" fresh-air supply pipe that is connected to your furnace.



In conjunction with a wall insulation upgrade, you may want to consider having the kitchen range hood fan exhaust to the outside.



The two bathroom fans on the main floor are older original units and you should consider upgrading them to ENERGY STAR rated units, that seal better, use much less electricity &amp; operate at low noise levels. Some newer bathroom fans have a built-in humidity sensor. Many homes now have timer switches connected to bathroom fans (where you select 5, 10, 15, 20 minutes) so these fans can remain on for an adequate time to remove bathroom moisture and/or odours without the risk of being left on all day and wasting heat.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Depressurization Test&lt;/b&gt;



The result of the exhaust devices depressurization test, which consists of turning on all exhaust equipment (the 3 bathroom fans, and the dryer, indicated a pressure drop of 5.6 Pascals. As this number is higher than 5 Pascals, the use of this equipment could cause combustion products to be drawn into your home, if you have any chimney vented appliances in your home that are not direct-vent. Your current furnace is a chimney vented model that is susceptible to back-drafting combustion gases into the house.



During my assessment of your home, there appeared to be functioning smoke or carbon monoxide (CO) detectors, however some appeared to be older - perhaps more than 10 years old ? Please ensure both types of detectors are always installed &amp; operational in your home, and not beyond their "replace by" date.



-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------



&lt;b&gt;Fuel Costs - Yellowknife&lt;/b&gt;



The following Nov. 1, 2017 values have been used to calculate energy costs and savings in Yellowknife:

Electricity - $19.45 min, $0.32 per kWh

Oil  - $1.12 per litre 

Propane - $0.71 per litre

Wood - $341 per cord delivered; $100 per cord self-harvested

Pellets - $5.67 per bag, $353.85 per tonne bulk delivered



